45417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1061 7792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f40eb50-1f7f-5b78-a04c-ac11239e4005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1061 7793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b00d4672-3ee3-5870-9ffd-2a7b3538ab54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1061 7794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5144de57-8e74-52bb-a605-38d854105198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1065 1609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aa49408-30d5-57b5-8dff-ec1744f9fc6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions. \n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22.$ We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1065 2050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cfd89526-b68e-5436-a4e1-e20aa477ab68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers. \n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1065 5995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4af2af2e-0f81-5094-adf6-2ede72a493d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{2}-6\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (4-6)\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1065 5996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32078f96-6eaf-57d2-bc71-8af8495d5d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075060 #edb/ref 1064 "A **triangle** is a geometric shape that consists of three points connected by three line segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0668279b79482db2fd78116a244bba8e28d3771f88d2e28fb4b6a62387f9d8fd.png)\n\nFor this triangle,\n\n- the line segments $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$ and $\\overline{CA}$ are the **sides** of the triangle,\n\n- the points $\\color{red}A$, $\\color{red}B$ and $\\color{red}C$ are the **vertices** of the triangle,\n\n- the angles $\\angle{A}$, $\\angle{B}$ and $\\angle{C}$ formed by the sides are called the **interior angles** of the triangle.\n\nTo denote a triangle, we use the symbol $\\boldsymbol{\\triangle}$ and list its vertices (in any order). So, the above diagram shows us \n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that all triangles have the following property:\n\n*The sum of the measures of the interior angles of any triangle is always equal to $180^\\circ.$*\n\nFor example, in $\\triangle ABC$ shown above, the interior angle theorem tells us that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A} + m\\angle{B} + m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ .\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we know the measures of two angles in a triangle, we can calculate the value of the other." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075060 #edb/ref 1065 9135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075060 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "96cce37a-34b0-5808-afbe-f145cca9fa52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075060 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1039 "Cross-Multiplying with One Binomial" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110619 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3ff2f965-b406-5c9a-bd41-73ccdce951eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1039 "Cross-Multiplying with Two Binomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110620 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e880edeb-82ad-5943-a846-dc3246e3e3bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1039 "Cross-Multiplying with a Negative Sign" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110621 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "59230000-ef0e-56c0-ab06-17676d817d5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross-Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110809 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "224e6883-ecf1-58d2-8217-7503c15c1910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111526 #edb/ref 1061 175966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62154097-0663-58a8-8dfe-b1f260a5583e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111527 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111527 #edb/ref 1061 175967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79e64205-d43a-5719-a973-65d5eb873b04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111528 #edb/ref 1061 175971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39ea7244-0ca1-52cc-9e51-81973115c977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111529 #edb/ref 1061 175974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5826411-42a0-5ce0-aa7f-6e0d021d40fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111530 #edb/ref 1061 175975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f42768c-0adb-5fc8-8dd3-b63229bed312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111911 #edb/ref 1064 "If we have rational expressions on both sides of an equation, say,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{red}x-1}}{{\\color{blue}2}} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}2x-1}}{{\\color{red}5}},\n$$\n\nthen we can eliminate the rational expressions by applying **cross-multiplication**:\n\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side numerator $({\\color{red}x-1})$ with the right-hand side denominator $({\\color{red}5}),$ and\n\n- we cross-multiply the left-hand side denominator $({\\color{blue}2})$ with the right-hand side numerator $({\\color{blue}2x-1}).$\n\nDoing this, we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}x-1}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}5}) = ({\\color{blue}2}) \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2x - 1})\n$$\n\nAfter we've cross-multiplied, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ 5x-5 & =4x-2 \\\\ 5x-5+5 & =4x-2+5 \\\\ 5x & =4x+3 \\\\ 5x-4x & =4x+3-4x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Cross-multiplication might seem magical, but it is really just a shortcut for multiplying both sides of the equation by each denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\frac{2x-1}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{2x-1}{5} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{2x-1}{5} \\\\ x-1 & =2\\cdot \\frac{2x-1}{5} \\\\ (x-1)\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot \\frac{2x-1}{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\ (x-1)\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot \\frac{2x-1}{\\cancel{5}}\\cdot \\cancel{5} \\\\ (x-1)\\cdot (5) & =(2)\\cdot (2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111911 #edb/ref 1065 8174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111911 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "99ff1453-e12b-518f-94b1-9baf8ee914e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111911 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111912 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{2} = -\\dfrac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nEven though one of the fractions is negative, we can still use cross-multiplication. All we have to do is transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{2} & =-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =\\frac{-x}{3} \\\\ (x+1)\\cdot 3 & =2\\cdot (-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)\\cdot 3 & =2\\cdot (-x) \\\\ 3x+3 & =-2x \\\\ 3x+3-3x & =-2x-3x \\\\ 3 & =-5x \\\\ \\frac{